## How the Shopping List works
// The Shopping List example keeps what to buy in a CSV file, and shows it by aisle:
// add an item, tick it off as you shop, clear what you got, and print what's left.
// Four ideas do the work:
// - one line says where the items are kept:  a CSV file in the example's own folder
// - an item draws itself as a row:  a box to tick bound to its "got"
// - the list is worked out from the items:  each aisle with what's still to get in it
// - "open printing" prints the app alone, and its CSS says what goes on paper
//
// This guide keeps the first three, with the items in memory:  they start afresh each run.
// Open Shopping List, from Examples on the home page, for all of it.


## An item
an "item" is a view
	its "name" is text
	its "amount" is a number
	its "unit" is text
	its "aisle" is text
	its "got" is yes or no
		// ticked off:  it's in the trolley

	to draw it
		// a form of its own, bound to the item:  ticking the box changes its "got"
		<form value={it}>
			<stack direction="horizontal" spacing="sm">
				<checkbox name="got" aria-label="Got it" />
				<text bold>{its amount && its unit}</text>
				<text>{its name}</text>
			</stack>
		</form>


## An aisle:  its name, and what's still to get in it
an "aisle" is a view
	its "name" is text
	its "items" is a list of items

	to draw it
		<stack spacing="xs">
			<header size="tiny">{its name}</header>
			<for item in its items />
		</stack>


## The app
shopping list is an app
	its "items" is a list of items
		// everything on the list, in memory here;  the example's are in its CSV file
	its "aisles" is
		// each aisle with something still to get in it, once, A to Z
		set found to a new list of aisles
		for item in (its items) sorted by its aisle
			if item's got is no
				set aisle to the first aisle in found where its name is the item's aisle
				if aisle does not exist
					set aisle to a new aisle with name = the item's aisle
					add aisle to found
				add item to the aisle's items
		return found

	to "clear what I got" with title "Clear what I got"
		// every ticked item off the list
		remove items from its items where its got is yes

	to draw it
		<container text>
			<stack>
				<action "clear what I got" />
				<for aisle in its aisles />
			</stack>
		</container>


## Start:  five things to buy, one already got
add (a new item with name "milk", amount = 2, unit = "litres", aisle "Dairy") to the items of the shopping list
add (a new item with name "eggs", amount = 12, aisle "Dairy") to the items of the shopping list
add (a new item with name "bananas", amount = 6, aisle "Fruit and veg") to the items of the shopping list
add (a new item with name "bread", amount = 1, unit = "loaf", aisle "Bakery") to the items of the shopping list
add (a new item with name "coffee", amount = 250, unit = "g", aisle "Drinks", got = yes) to the items of the shopping list


## A check
to test the aisles
	set list to a new shopping list
	add (a new item with name "milk", aisle "Dairy") to list's items
	add (a new item with name "eggs", aisle "Dairy") to list's items
	add (a new item with name "apples", aisle "Fruit", got = yes) to list's items
	the length of list's aisles should be 1


## Try it
// - Tick "milk":  it leaves Dairy, which still has the eggs.
// - Press "Clear what I got":  the coffee goes for good.
// - Give an item an aisle of its own, "Frozen", and see a new heading.
//
// Where next:  Shopping List, from Examples on the home page, which keeps its items in a CSV file.


## Where the example keeps its items
// This guide's items live in memory:  reload, and they start again.
// Shopping List keeps them in a CSV file, in its own folder:  __data/shopping.csv.
// One line, in its API file, says so:
//       shopping sheet is a sheet at "shopping.csv"
// and an item says it lives there:
//       it lives in shopping sheet
// The page server writes the file, changing only the lines that change:  open it in Numbers or Excel and see.
// After those lines, the words you already know reach the file:
//       add (a new item with ...) to the items
//       every item where its got is no
//       remove items from the items where its got is yes
// Recipe Cards imports that API file, so the ingredients it adds land in the same file, on the same list.


## On paper
// Shopping List's Print button runs one step:
//       open printing
// which opens the browser's Print dialog for the app alone, not the editor around it.
// Its CSS says what's on paper:  only what's still to get, each with its box to tick.
//       @media print {
//         ui-toolbar, .new-item, .got, .item ui-button { display: none; }
//       }
